Q: IBM Liberty JakartaEE11 version artifacts A: <p>OpenLiberty Jakarta EE 11 beta is are currently available as 26.0.0.x-beta here: <a href="https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/io.openliberty.beta/openliberty-runtime" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/io.openliberty.beta/openliberty-runtime</a></p>
<p>NOTE: <code>&lt;feature&gt;jakartaee-11.0&lt;/feature&gt;</code> for <code>server.xml</code> is not yet available in current ones. For now, you need to explicitly specify the individual EE11 artifacts you wish to use, whenever available in the current beta version.</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;featureManager&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;localConnector-1.0&lt;/feature&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;servlet-6.1&lt;/feature&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;expressionLanguage-6.0&lt;/feature&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;cdi-4.1&lt;/feature&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;faces-4.1&lt;/feature&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;websocket-2.2&lt;/feature&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;validation-3.1&lt;/feature&gt;
    &lt;feature&gt;jsonp-2.1&lt;/feature&gt;
&lt;/featureManager&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>See also <a href="https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/blob/5.x/src/test/resources/arquillian/liberty/server.xml" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/omnifaces/omnifaces/blob/5.x/src/test/resources/arquillian/liberty/server.xml</a></p>

Q: How do I do sign in with apple ID with json and revoke token when user wants to permanently deactivate account in flutter/dart? A: <h2>Option A: Revoke directly from the client using Firebase Authentication (easiest)</h2>
<p>If you use <strong>Firebase Authentication</strong>, you can invalidate the tokens and associated user authorizations for a user <strong>directly from the client app</strong>. Firebase for Flutter exposes a helper API that safely handles Apple’s revoke flow for you.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>UserCredential userCredential = await FirebaseAuth.instance.signInWithPopup(appleProvider);
// Keep the authorization code returned from Apple platforms
String? authCode = userCredential.additionalUserInfo?.authorizationCode;
// Revoke Apple auth token
await FirebaseAuth.instance.revokeTokenWithAuthorizationCode(authCode!);
</code></pre>
<p>✔ No own backend logic required
✔ Secure (no Apple developer account secrets exposed)</p>
<p>📖 Official Firebase documentation:
👉 <strong><a href="https://firebase.google.com/docs/auth/flutter/federated-auth#revoke-apple" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Revoke Apple auth tokens</a></strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Option B: Revoke via your own backend (more flexible, full control)</h2>
<p>If you need <strong>custom flows</strong> (multi-provider unlinking, audits, enterprise logic, etc.), you can revoke Apple tokens yourself, <strong>but only from a server</strong>. This can be implemented using Firebase Cloud Functions, Supabase Edge Functions, or any secure backend.</p>
<p>Reason: Apple requires a <code>client_secret</code> via Apple developer account secrets, which must <strong>never</strong> live in client apps.</p>
<h3>Server-side token revocation for Sign in with Apple:</h3>
<h4>1. Depending on your platform and Firebase usage, you may get either an <strong>authorization code</strong> or an <strong>access token</strong>:</h4>
<ul>
<li>Flutter Firebase Authentication on <strong>iOS</strong>: <code>.reauthenticateWithProvider(appleProvider)</code> returns an <strong>authorization code</strong></li>
<li>Flutter Firebase Authentication on <strong>Android and Web</strong>: the same call returns an <strong>access token</strong> directly</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. If you have an <strong>authorization code</strong>, exchange it for an access token on your backend:</h4>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const response = await axios.post(&quot;https://appleid.apple.com/auth/token&quot;, {
  client_id: clientId,
  client_secret: clientSecret,
  code: authorizationCode,
  grant_type: &quot;authorization_code&quot;,
}, {
  headers: {&quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/x-www-form-urlencoded&quot;},
});
accessToken = response.data.access_token;
</code></pre>
<h4>3. Once you have the <strong>access token</strong>, revoke it on your backend:</h4>
<p>Please be aware that for the <code>client_id</code>, you should use the <strong>Apple App ID</strong> for access tokens retrieved via iOS, and the <strong>Apple Service ID</strong> via Android and Web.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>await axios.post(&quot;https://appleid.apple.com/auth/revoke&quot;, {
  client_id: clientId,
  client_secret: clientSecret,
  token: accessToken,
  token_type_hint: &quot;access_token&quot;,
}, {
  headers: {&quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/x-www-form-urlencoded&quot;},
});
</code></pre>
<p>📦 Full working example (end-to-end Apple revoke flow):
👉 <strong><a href="https://github.com/alphia-gmbh/flutter-package-core/blob/main/example/functions/index.js" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GitHub example for backend logic</a></strong>
👉 <strong><a href="https://github.com/alphia-gmbh/flutter-package-core/blob/main/lib/service_auth.dart" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GitHub example for client logic</a></strong></p>
<hr />
<p>Hope this clears things up 👍</p>
